// PassPipeline — the stateful driver for a compilation. It owns the evolving IR
// modules (one per tier), the scheduled pass list, and a `dispatch` that runs each
// pass by reading one module field and writing the next. Callers build a
// PassPipeline, push the passes they want via `addPass`/`addPasses`, and call
// `run()`.
//
// Pipeline phases (3-level IR: Maxon → Std → Target):
//   Phase 2  Maxon-level  : resolveTypes, semanticCheck  (operate on maxonModule)
//   Phase 3  Maxon → Std  : lowerMaxonToStd              (maxonModule → stdModule)
//   Phase 7  Std → Target : handled directly by the backend (not pipeline passes)
//
// PHI RESOLUTION moved OUT of the pipeline at M5: the register allocator consumes
// `blockArgs`/`branchEdges` DIRECTLY and does SSA destruction after coloring, so
// the M4b `eliminatePhis` Std pass is retired (it eagerly inserted edge copies and
// cleared the phi model before the backend, which the biased colorer needs intact).
//
// There is NO MIR tier — see StdDialect's header. v1's `lowerStdToMir` pass and
// `mirModule` field are gone, and everything v1 ran ON the MIR tier (instruction
// scheduling, coalescing, and the desugaring that becomes `lowerToMachineForm`)
// becomes a Std-tier pass scheduled here.
//
// THIN for maxon-shv2 M1: only the passes the `return 42` slice needs are
// scheduled, and only `lowerMaxonToStd` does real work. resolveTypes /
// semanticCheck are SCHEDULED PLACEHOLDERS whose bodies are a Chunk C deliverable
// (TypeResolution + SemanticCheck) — present now so the pipeline SHAPE is stable
// and Chunk C fills in bodies rather than rewiring the pipeline. The Std-tier
// optimization passes (mem2reg/canonicalize/cse/…), the Own-tier passes, and
// `lowerToMachineForm` GROW in at their milestones (M3+ / M6). Error gating
// (`hasErrors` → throw) and per-pass IR-compaction/dump grow in with the Project +
// diagnostics (Chunk C).

// One arm per scheduled pass. Payload-free today; kept a `union` (not an enum)
// to match v1 and leave room for passes that carry configuration payloads.
export union PassKind
	// Phase 2: Maxon-level. Chunk C bodies (placeholders in M1).
	resolveTypes
	semanticCheck
	// Phase 3: Maxon → Std.
	lowerMaxonToStd
	// Phase 3.4: Std → Std ranged-typealias narrowing guards (P1.9). Turns the parser's recorded
	// range-check sites into compile-time E3005s and runtime panic guards. It runs right after the
	// lowering (value ids are freshest and unrenumbered) and BEFORE `foldConstOperands`, which then
	// folds each guard's bound `const` into the `cmp`'s immediate. Appended after `lowerMaxonToStd` so
	// the existing `pruneDeadBlockArgs to foldConstOperands` union range still names the three
	// block-arg/const passes exactly.
	insertRangeChecks
	// Phase 3.5: Std → Std machine-form rewrites.
	//
	// `pruneDeadBlockArgs` deletes the loop-header phis nothing ever reads — a var the loop
	// ASSIGNS (so the parser carries a phi for it) but whose phi has no reader. It runs FIRST of the Std passes because a dead phi is
	// SELF-SUSTAINING — its back edge keeps it live around the whole loop — so it inflates
	// the register allocator's maxlive and can raise a FALSE E5001; and because dropping
	// its edge args is what lets `foldConstOperands`'s const DCE (below) then drop a
	// `const` whose only reader was that edge.
	//
	// `elimTrivialBlockArgs` folds away the phis that CARRY NOTHING — one whose every
	// incoming value, self-references discounted, is the same value. Those are the phis the
	// parser's token scan OVER-approximates into existence: a `var`/`let` declared inside the
	// loop that shadows an outer var of the same name reads as an assignment to the outer one.
	// They are not dead (something reads them), so `pruneDeadBlockArgs` leaves
	// them; but a PHI is exactly what the cold-spill splitter may not spill, so an idle `var`
	// across a loop was pinned in a register where an idle `let` was spilled for free — and
	// past the pool, a FALSE E5001. It runs after the prune (which is cheaper and removes the
	// self-sustaining dead phis first) and before `foldConstOperands`, whose const DCE can then
	// drop a `const` whose only reader was a folded phi's edge.
	//
	// `foldConstOperands` moves a const operand into an immediate so the literal never
	// needs a register.
	pruneDeadBlockArgs
	elimTrivialBlockArgs
	foldConstOperands
	// Phase 3.6: Std → Std ESCAPE ANALYSIS + STACK PROMOTION (`PromoteStackRecords`).
	//
	// ⭐ **IT RUNS LAST OF THE Std PASSES, AND EVERY ONE OF THEM IS A PRECONDITION IT READS.** The
	// promotion decides on the SHAPE of the code, so it must see the final shape:
	// `pruneDeadBlockArgs`/`elimTrivialBlockArgs` remove the phis the parser over-approximated into
	// existence, and a record reaching a phi is refused — so running before them would refuse
	// promotions on merges that do not survive. `insertRangeChecks` may SPLIT a constructor's block to
	// hang a guard off it, and a multi-block body is not a record constructor — so running before it
	// would recognize a constructor the guard is about to invalidate.
	//
	// It is the only WHOLE-MODULE pass in this list (see `classifyPass`): a call site cannot be judged
	// without the callee's summary.
	promoteStackRecords
end 'PassKind'

export typealias PassKindArray = Array with PassKind

// Whether a pass runs over the whole module at once or can fan out per function.
// `perFunction` passes are intra-function only (no pass reaches into another
// function's blocks); they will run through the per-function fan-out driver
// added at M5. `wholeModule` passes need call-graph / cross-function visibility.
export enum PassClassification
	wholeModule
	perFunction
end 'PassClassification'

// Map a pass to its fan-out classification. resolveTypes / semanticCheck are
// whole-module (they need the full declaration set); the lowering is
// intra-function and becomes the first fan-out target at M5. NOTE: M1's `run()`
// runs EVERY pass whole-module regardless — this classification is recorded for
// the M5 fan-out driver, not yet acted on (per PLAN.md: classification present
// but trivial at M1).
export function classifyPass(pass PassKind) returns PassClassification
	return match pass 'classify'
		resolveTypes gives PassClassification.wholeModule
		semanticCheck gives PassClassification.wholeModule
		lowerMaxonToStd gives PassClassification.perFunction
		insertRangeChecks gives PassClassification.perFunction
		pruneDeadBlockArgs gives PassClassification.perFunction
		elimTrivialBlockArgs gives PassClassification.perFunction
		foldConstOperands gives PassClassification.perFunction
		// The one whole-module Std pass: the constructor table and the parameter-escape summary are facts
		// about the CALL GRAPH, and a call site cannot be judged without the callee's row.
		promoteStackRecords gives PassClassification.wholeModule
	end 'classify'
end 'classifyPass'

// The wall-clock phase each pass is billed to. EXHAUSTIVE by construction: a new
// PassKind fails to compile until it names its phase, so a pass cannot be added and
// silently go unmeasured — which is the whole point of the attribution (see
// CompileTimings' header). The mapping is 1:1 today and is expected to stay so; a
// pass grouping several phases would defeat the table's purpose.
export function compilePhaseOfPass(pass PassKind) returns CompilePhase
	return match pass 'phase'
		resolveTypes gives CompilePhase.resolveTypes
		semanticCheck gives CompilePhase.semanticCheck
		lowerMaxonToStd gives CompilePhase.lowerMaxonToStd
		insertRangeChecks gives CompilePhase.insertRangeChecks
		pruneDeadBlockArgs gives CompilePhase.pruneDeadBlockArgs
		elimTrivialBlockArgs gives CompilePhase.elimTrivialBlockArgs
		foldConstOperands gives CompilePhase.foldConstOperands
		promoteStackRecords gives CompilePhase.promoteStackRecords
	end 'phase'
end 'compilePhaseOfPass'

// Human-readable pass name for logging (union has no `.name`, unlike an enum).
export function passKindToString(pass PassKind) returns String
	return match pass 'name'
		resolveTypes gives "resolveTypes"
		semanticCheck gives "semanticCheck"
		lowerMaxonToStd gives "lowerMaxonToStd"
		insertRangeChecks gives "insertRangeChecks"
		pruneDeadBlockArgs gives "pruneDeadBlockArgs"
		elimTrivialBlockArgs gives "elimTrivialBlockArgs"
		foldConstOperands gives "foldConstOperands"
		promoteStackRecords gives "promoteStackRecords"
	end 'name'
end 'passKindToString'

// ⭐⭐ **THE PIPELINE IS SCHEDULED IN TWO STAGES, AND THE SEAM BETWEEN THEM IS A HARD IR BOUNDARY, NOT A
// CONVENIENCE.** Everything up to and including `semanticCheck` operates on the MAXON module and may still
// rewrite it; `lowerMaxonToStd` and everything after it operate on the Std one. The driver derives the
// program's RUNTIME NEEDS and lays out `.data` in the seam, because that derivation is a walk of the FINAL
// Maxon module and both of its neighbours' positions are forced:
//
//   • it must run AFTER `semanticCheck`, because `promoteArgsNeedingAWritableRecord` inserts `__str_clone` /
//     `__str_retain` / `__str_decref` calls there, and a runtime entry the scan walked past is one nothing
//     installs — an unresolved relocation;
//   • it must run BEFORE `lowerMaxonToStd`, and that is not a preference. **MEASURED:** the lowering pushes
//     the SAME `IrFunction` objects into the Std module (`transferFuncs`) and then `stdModule.addBlock`s
//     onto their shared `blockRefs`, so after it runs a Maxon function's block refs name STD blocks — a
//     Maxon-module walk then panics on `blockRef … not in module.blocks`, which is exactly what a scan
//     placed below the whole pipeline did, on seven cases.
//
// The Std-tier passes GROW into the SECOND stage, appended after `lowerMaxonToStd`: the optimizations
// (mem2reg/canonicalize/cse/… at M3+), the Own-tier refcount passes (M6), and `lowerToMachineForm` (M6) —
// which in v1 would have been a separate "backend pipeline" over the MIR tier. Phase 7 (target lowering /
// regalloc / prologue-epilogue) stays backend-direct: it produces a CodeResult, which is not an IR module
// and so does not fit the module-field dispatch model.
//
// (v1 threads `project` here for `coverageEnabled`; M1's passes do not need it.)
export function buildMaxonTierPasses() returns PassKindArray
	var passes = PassKindArray.create()
	passes.push(PassKind.resolveTypes)
	passes.push(PassKind.semanticCheck)
	return passes
end 'buildMaxonTierPasses'

// The second stage — see `buildMaxonTierPasses` for what the seam between them is for.
export function buildLoweringPasses() returns PassKindArray
	var passes = PassKindArray.create()
	passes.push(PassKind.lowerMaxonToStd)
	passes.push(PassKind.insertRangeChecks)
	passes.push(PassKind.pruneDeadBlockArgs)
	passes.push(PassKind.elimTrivialBlockArgs)
	passes.push(PassKind.foldConstOperands)
	passes.push(PassKind.promoteStackRecords)
	return passes
end 'buildLoweringPasses'

// The pipeline owns the IR modules across their successive phases. It starts with
// `maxonModule` populated by the parser (Chunk C); the lowering pass reads one
// field and populates the next. Unpopulated fields hold empty modules so a read
// never traps. Pass methods read/write `self`'s fields directly — new
// cross-cutting state becomes a new field, not a signature change.
//
// It owns NO target module: the pipeline's last tier is Std, and the backend is
// driven backend-direct (`buildBackend` returns a CodeResult, which is not an IR
// module). A `targetModule` field here would be write-once-read-never — the same
// vestigial-tier residue the `mirModule` field was.
export type PassPipeline
	// The project owns the shared state the Maxon-tier passes read/write
	// (interner, registries, diagnostics). resolveTypes/semanticCheck need it, and since P1.6-B1 the
	// lowering does too — it materialises a `sizeof(T)` receiver's layout descriptor from the project's
	// struct/generic-instance registries; every pass dispatches uniformly through `self` regardless.
	export var project as Project
	export var maxonModule as MaxonModule
	export var stdModule as StdModule
	// The whole-program declaration index. `semanticCheck` reads its ENUM registry (E3113, A1s-throwsbox):
	// `Parser.throwsTypeIsBoxedUnion` — the catch-site DECODE that decides whether an error flag is an ordinal
	// or a box pointer — resolves a `throws` clause through `ProgramSignatures.enumOf`, so the check that a
	// clause names something classifiable has to ask THAT registry and not the `Project.enumTypes` authority
	// beside it. The two are fed identically today (one `createEnumRegistry`, the same declarations); asking
	// the other one and calling it "the decode's registry" would be this project's signature bug — one fact
	// written down twice — waiting for the day they diverge.
	//
	// It is a FIELD rather than an argument threaded through `dispatch` because that is this type's stated
	// arrangement for cross-cutting state, and because the query is memoized: `compileToCodeResult` has
	// already forced it, so binding it here costs a reference and no work.
	export var signatures as ProgramSignatures
	// ⭐ **THE CHECKED NAME INDEX, BUILT ONCE FOR BOTH STAGES.** It is a FIELD rather than a `run`-local
	// because the pipeline is now RUN TWICE — the Maxon-tier stage, then the lowering stage, with the
	// runtime-needs derivation in the seam (`buildMaxonTierPasses`) — and one index is exactly what its own
	// header claims: the Maxon function array is frozen across the whole pipeline, so a second build would
	// re-derive an identical answer and re-run its duplicate refusal at a point nothing can have changed.
	export var funcNames as FunctionNameIndex
	// ⭐⭐ **DOES THIS PROGRAM RUN GREEN THREADS?** — the one fact `promoteStackRecords` cannot derive from
	// the Std module, and the one that decides whether STACK PROMOTION is legal at all in this compile.
	//
	// A green thread's stack GROWS BY RELOCATION: `__gt_morestack` allocates a larger region, COPIES the
	// old stack onto it, rebases rsp and walks the saved-rbp chain adding the offset — and then FREES the
	// old pages (X64GtRuntime, "copy the stack + rebase the live rbp + WALK the saved-rbp chain"). Every
	// frame-relative access survives that, because rsp and rbp are exactly what it fixes. A frame ADDRESS
	// materialized into a REGISTER does not: nothing enumerates the registers, so a promoted record's
	// pointer held across a call whose prologue guard fires morestack would afterwards point into freed
	// pages. `TargetOp.leaRegSlot` is the FIRST op in shv2 that materializes one, so this hazard is
	// exactly as old as stack promotion.
	//
	// The bit is `RuntimeUsage.usesGt` — the SAME authority that decides whether the scheduler is installed
	// at all, rather than a second guess at "does this program use async" derived from the op stream.
	// Written in the seam between the two pipeline stages (`Compiler.compileToCodeResult`), which is where
	// `scanRuntimeUsage` has just answered it and before any Std pass runs.
	export var usesGreenThreads as bool

	// ⚠ **IT THROWS, BECAUSE BUILDING THE NAME INDEX IS THE COMPILE'S DUPLICATE-FUNCTION-NAME REFUSAL**
	// (E4015 / `refuseDuplicateFunctionName`). The gate used to be `run`'s; with two runs it belongs to the
	// one construction they share, and the driver wraps it in the same `refuseCompile` it wraps `run` in.
	// Nothing is refused later than before: the index was already built ahead of `resolveTypes`, which is
	// still the first pass to report anything.
	export static function create(project Project, maxonModule MaxonModule, signatures ProgramSignatures) returns PassPipeline throws CompileError
		// ⭐⭐ **ONE CHECKED NAME INDEX FOR THE WHOLE PIPELINE (A2l).** Three passes derive a name-keyed map
		// from this module's function array — `semanticCheck`'s throws map and escape summary, and
		// `lowerMaxonToStd`'s layout-descriptor set — and each used to hand-roll its own bare `upsert` loop.
		// Two of them ran BEFORE the pass that built the checking index, so they were right by ORDERING and
		// could not say so; the third had no index in front of it at all.
		//
		// It is built HERE, and once, because the Maxon function array is FROZEN across this pipeline: every
		// synthesizer that appends to it (`installManagedGlobalInit` is the last) has run in
		// `compileToCodeResult` before the pipeline is even constructed, `resolveTypes` rewrites signature
		// FIELDS in place without touching the array, and `lowerMaxonToStd` pushes into the STD module it
		// produces. So one index describes the array every pass below will walk — which is what lets each of
		// them take the index instead of the module, and stop resting on a guarantee none of them stated.
		//
		// It replaces `buildParamMutationSummary`'s build rather than adding one, so the duplicate refusal
		// simply moves EARLIER — ahead of `resolveTypes` instead of the middle of `semanticCheck`.
		//
		// ⚠ **IT USED TO BE BILLED TO `phase:semanticCheck`, AND THEN TO NOTHING AT ALL.** Its SIZE is no
		// longer written down here, because it is now a row: `phase:pipelineNameIndex` in `--metrics`, read
		// off the compile being asked about instead of remembered from one. What is worth keeping is the
		// SHAPE, which a single row cannot show — MEASURED at A2l's review (2026-07-31, by temporarily
		// billing this statement to the always-empty `emitIr` slot and reading the scale corpus): rung 0 at
		// 47 allocations, rung 5 at 79, i.e. **×1.68 across a 32× ladder**. That is the MAP's geometric
		// buffer growth rather than a per-entry cost, which is why it does not track the program.
		//
		// **It cannot go inside a pass's bracket, so it has one of its own.** `dispatch` opens one bracket
		// PER PASS, while this exists before any of them and is read by several, so billing it to one would
		// make that pass's row mean two things — the defect `CompileTimings`' header exists to prevent.
		//
		// ⭐ **AND THE PHASE IS NAMED `pipelineNameIndex`, NOT `nameIndex`, BECAUSE A ROW MUST NOT CLAIM MORE
		// THAN IT BRACKETS.** There are THREE Maxon-tier `FunctionNameIndex.create` calls; this is one of
		// them, and a row spelled `nameIndex` would under-report the mechanism threefold. That objection is
		// what kept this statement unbracketed until A2n, and it is answered by the NAME rather than by
		// leaving the cost in a remainder that `ScaleTestRunner` reads and discards. The other two —
		// `ConformanceCheck.checkConformance`'s and `deriveStdlibFacts`' — are now inside
		// `CompilePhase.checkDeclarations` and `CompilePhase.deriveRuntimeNeeds`, billed to the passes whose
		// own cost they are.
		let nameIndexProbe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.pipelineNameIndex)
		let funcNames = try FunctionNameIndex.create(maxonModule.functions, project: project)
		recordPhase(project, phase: CompilePhase.pipelineNameIndex, probe: nameIndexProbe)

		return Self{
			project: project,
			maxonModule: maxonModule,
			stdModule: StdModule.create(),
			signatures: signatures,
			funcNames: funcNames,
			usesGreenThreads: false
		}
	end 'create'

	// Run `passes` in order, gating on diagnostics after each: once a
	// pass reports an error (semanticCheck's E3001/E3002), the pipeline throws
	// rather than lower an invalid module (e.g. one with no `main` for the entry
	// stub to call). The driver catches this, prints the diagnostics, and exits
	// non-zero. Per-pass IR compaction/dump grows in with the backend cache.
	//
	// The schedule is an ARGUMENT rather than accumulated state because the driver runs the pipeline in two
	// stages and each stage is one list — see `buildMaxonTierPasses` for what sits between them.
	export function run(passes PassKindArray) throws CompileError
		for pass in passes 'eachPass'
			logDebug(LogCategory.ir, message: function() gives "=== Running pass: {passKindToString(pass)} ===")
			self.dispatch(pass)
			if projectHasErrors(self.project) 'errorGate'
				throw CompileError.compileError
			end 'errorGate'
		end 'eachPass'
	end 'run'

	// One arm per PassKind. Each reads the incoming module off `self`, runs the
	// standalone pass algorithm, and writes the result back. The pass algorithms
	// stay free functions (reusable outside the pipeline); dispatch just wires
	// them up. resolveTypes/semanticCheck operate on the Maxon module + project;
	// lowerMaxonToStd produces the next tier's module.
	//
	// Measurement lives at THIS choke point rather than inside each pass body: every pass
	// runs through here, so no pass can be scheduled and go unmeasured, and the passes
	// stay free functions that know nothing about the clock or the allocator. No pass
	// calls another, so the phases are disjoint with nothing to subtract.
	// The name index comes off `self` rather than riding a parameter: it is the PIPELINE's, built once for
	// both stages, and threading it would have made a per-run copy of a fact the type already owns.
	function dispatch(pass PassKind)
		let probe = PhaseProbe.beginPhase(self.project, phase: compilePhaseOfPass(pass))

		match pass 'dispatch'
			resolveTypes then resolveTypes(self.project, module: self.maxonModule)
			semanticCheck then semanticCheck(self.project, module: self.maxonModule, signatures: self.signatures, funcNames: self.funcNames)
			lowerMaxonToStd then self.stdModule = lowerMaxonToStd(self.maxonModule, project: self.project, funcNames: self.funcNames)
			insertRangeChecks then insertRangeChecks(self.project, module: self.stdModule)
			pruneDeadBlockArgs then pruneDeadBlockArgs(self.stdModule)
			elimTrivialBlockArgs then elimTrivialBlockArgs(self.stdModule)
			foldConstOperands then foldConstOperands(self.stdModule)
			promoteStackRecords then promoteStackRecords(self.project, module: self.stdModule, usesGreenThreads: self.usesGreenThreads)
		end 'dispatch'

		recordPhase(self.project, phase: compilePhaseOfPass(pass), probe: probe)
	end 'dispatch'
end 'PassPipeline'
